1          /* SAS program to accompany the articles by Rick Wicklin on The DO Loop:
2             "Cohen's D statistic in SAS" (11AUG2025)
3             https://blogs.sas.com/content/iml/2025/08/11/cohens-d-statistic-in-sas.html
4             and
5             "Confidence intervals for Cohen's d statistic in SAS" (18AUG2025)
6             https://blogs.sas.com/content/iml/2025/08/18/cohens-d-ci-in-sas.html
7          
8             This program shows how to compute estimates of the effect size for a
9             standardized mean difference (SMD) in a two-sample independent design.
10            The estimates are
11                Cohen's d
12                Hedges' g
13            For each statistic, we compute a standard error and a
14            confidence interval for the SMD.
15         
16            The best overview of effect size statistics is
17              Goulet-Pelletier & Cousineau (2018)Ã¢â‚¬â€¹
18              https://www.tqmp.org/RegularArticles/vol14-4/p242/p242.pdf
19            When you read the paper, you will learn:
20              1. There are many different estimates for the SMD.
21              2. There are many different formulas for the standard error.
22              3.  There are several ways to estimate a confidence interval.
                                                           The SAS System

23            In the paper, G-P & C state:
24              1. "HedgesÃ¢â‚¬â„¢ g should always be preferred over CohenÃ¢â‚¬â„¢s d" (p. 245) and
25                 "Hedges g is superior to the other estimators" (p. 255).
26              2. The best estimate of SE is "True SE."
27              3. CIs obtained from noncentral t distribution are exact for Hedges' g
28                 if the true noncentrality parameter is known.
29                 "The noncentral CI is without a doubt the most reliable method especially
30                  when n is smaller than 20." G-P & C (p. 251).
31                 "The noncentral method is superior to the central method" (p. 255).
32            This program shows how to compute both Cohen's d and Hedges' g, as well as the
33            recommended formulas for SE and CI.It uses only Base SAS procedures:
34            PROC TTEST and the DATA step.
35         */
